hypotheses.statement:
The coefficients in <a href="#coef:b" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="coef:b">[coef:b]</a>–<a href="#coef:c" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="coef:c">[coef:c]</a> satisfy $$\begin{aligned}
 \sum_{j<J}b_j&=-4\pi a^2\log r_J+o(\log r_J),\label{coef:bsum}\\
 \sum_{j<J}c_{j,q}(y)&=\binom q2\frac{\pi a^2}{2}\log r_J
                         +o(\log r_J),\label{coef:csum}
\end{aligned}$$ where the second error is uniform in $y$ for each fixed $q$.

Along every tuned trajectory obtained above, $$\label{flow:variance-asymptotic}
 g_j\sim\frac1{B_0j}\quad(j\to\infty),\qquad
 \sum_{j\ge0}|g_{j+1}-g_j|\le C\bar g.$$

For each $q\in\mathcal Q$ and sufficiently small $D>0$, $A_{j,q}>0$ and there are fixed constants $C_*,B_s,C$ such that, at every scale, $$\begin{aligned}
 \left\lVert u_j\right\rVert_{v,q}&\le C_*A_{j,q}^{1/q},\label{mark:lq}\\
 \bigl(\mathbb E\left\lVert u_{j,i}\right\rVert^q\left\lVert h_{j,m}\right\rVert^p\bigr)^{1/p}
 &\le (C_*A_{j,q}^{1/q})^{q/p}B_s s_j
       e^{-vq n_i/p-vn_m}.\label{mark:mixed}
\end{aligned}$$ For $j\ge1$ one has the stronger estimate $$\label{mark:kernel-small}
 \left\lVert u_j-\alpha_jw_{j,y}\right\rVert_{v,q}
       \le C A_{j,q}^{1/q}s_j.$$

For $j\ge1$, with the bounded formal profile $Z_{j,q,y}^*$ from Section <a href="#sec:coefficients" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:coefficients">6</a>, $$\begin{aligned}
 C_{2,j}&=O(A_js_j^3),\label{mark:c2}\\
 C_{1,j}&=A_jg_j Z_{j,q,y}^*
       +O\bigl(A_jG_j(s_j+\rho^j)\bigr),\label{mark:c1}\\
 S_j&=A_jw_{j,y}^{\otimes q}+O(A_js_j).\label{mark:s-profile}
\end{aligned}$$ Consequently the marked charge moments obey $$\label{mark:scalar-recursion}
 A_{j+1}=A_j\left(1+c_{j,q}(y)g_j+
                    O\bigl(G_j(s_j+\rho^j)\bigr)\right).$$

For every fixed $t'<0$ in the parameter window and sufficiently small fixed $D>0$, $$\label{crit:subcritical-decay}
 \mathbb E\phi^{\mathrm w}_{t',\omega}
                  (0\longleftrightarrow\partial\Lambda_n)
                      \le C_{t',D}n^{-6}.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
Assume the deterministic reference-model estimates of [[ReferenceIsing](#ref-ReferenceIsing)], at the scopes stated in Section <a href="#sec:reference" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:reference">2</a>. There is $\varepsilon_0>0$ such that for every fixed $\varepsilon\in(0,\varepsilon_0)$, $$\label{eq:main-limit}
 \lim_{r\to\infty,\,r\in\mathbb N}
       \frac{\log R_\varepsilon(r)}{\log\log r}=\frac14.$$ The critical inverse temperature in <a href="#eq:quenched-correlation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:quenched-correlation">[eq:quenched-correlation]</a> is the physical value <a href="#eq:critical-definition" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:critical-definition">[eq:critical-definition]</a>; it is the unique positive solution of $$\label{eq:critical-curve}
 \sinh\bigl(2\beta(1-\varepsilon)\bigr)
 \sinh\bigl(2\beta(1+\varepsilon)\bigr)=1.$$

Fix $\nu>0$ smaller than $p-b$ in the applicable row of <a href="#ref:exponents" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ref:exponents">[ref:exponents]</a>. For all sufficiently large $w$ and $R\ge Cw$, a function $F$ of that parity, supported in $B_w(z)$, satisfies $$\begin{aligned}
 \left\lVert P_{R,z}F^\circ\right\rVert_\infty
   &\le C(w/R)^b\left\lVert F\right\rVert_\infty,\label{ref:ordinary-decay}\\
 \left\lVert P_{R,z}F^\circ-\ell_b(F)P_{R,z}\mathcal O_b(z)\right\rVert_\infty
   &\le C(w/R)^{p-\nu}\left\lVert F\right\rVert_\infty.
   \label{ref:improved-decay}
\end{aligned}$$ In particular, the second bound is the improved decay bound for a function of charge zero. We shall use $\nu=1/4$. The exponent $3-\nu$ requires the individual even function to be invariant under right-angle rotations; invariance of its probability law alone does not suffice. For a site at fixed positive relative distance from the cutting contour, $$\label{ref:microscopic-decay}
 \left\lVert P_{R,z}e(y)\right\rVert_\infty\le CR^{-1},\qquad
 \left\lVert P_{R,z}\sigma_y\right\rVert_\infty\le CR^{-d}.$$ The first bound also holds for a single plane-centered bond product.

Consider a finite critical reference graph in a square chart. Let $I,J$ and $V_{\mathrm{pin}}$ be pairwise disjoint vertex sets, and prescribe common spin values on $V_{\mathrm{pin}}$. Suppose $I\subset B_w(z)$ and $J\subset B_R(z)^c$. For $R\ge(1+c)w$ with fixed $c>0$, every assignment $a$ on $I$ and assignments $\tau,\tau'$ on $J$ satisfy $$\label{ref:density-ratio}
 C_c^{-1}\le
 \frac{\mathbb P_0(\sigma_I=a\mid\sigma_J=\tau,\sigma_{V_{\mathrm{pin}}})}
      {\mathbb P_0(\sigma_I=a\mid\sigma_J=\tau',\sigma_{V_{\mathrm{pin}}})}
 \le C_c.$$ For $R\ge8w\ge8$, the absolute logarithm of this ratio is at most $C(w/R)^\theta$ for some $\theta>0$. The constants are uniform in mesh, common pins, and auxiliary finite real fields. Either law can be replaced by a mixture; the comparison also holds after a common conditional randomization, across fixed polygonal collars of positive relative width, and for different continuations whose reference interactions agree in the separating chart. Free or fixed physical boundaries can intersect such collars.*


*In particular, let $W>0$ and $f$ be bounded functions supported in $B_w(z)$, let $s/w>1$ leave a fixed relative collar, and put $$\label{ref:normalized-weights}
 Z=\langle W\rangle_0,\qquad
 N=Z^{-1}P_{s,z}W,\qquad M=Z^{-1}P_{s,z}(fW).$$ Then $$\label{ref:weight-bounds}
 C^{-1}\le N\le C,\qquad \left\lVert M\right\rVert_\infty\le C\left\lVert f\right\rVert_\infty,
 \qquad \langle N\rangle_0=1,\quad
 \langle M\rangle_0=\frac{\langle fW\rangle_0}{Z}.$$ The bounds are independent of the amplitude of $W$. If $W$ and $f$ are spin-flip even, then $N$ and $M$ are even.

Fix concentric inner and outer squares with a sufficiently large fixed ratio of radii and fixed relative buffers. Rescale the inner radius to one; the resulting mesh is $1/w$ when the original radius is $w$ in lattice units. For every $\eta>0$ there are an integer $N$ and a fine-mesh threshold such that, at each finer mesh, one can choose outer cutting configurations $\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_N$ with $$\label{ref:finite-tests}
 \left\lVert PF\right\rVert_\infty
      \le \max_{1\le k\le N}|PF(\tau_k)|+\eta\left\lVert F\right\rVert_\infty$$ for every bounded function $F$ supported in the inner square. Here $P$ averages to the outer contour. The number $N$ is independent of the mesh and of $F$; the actual configurations may depend on the mesh. Consequently, for a random inner function $F$ and $q\ge1$, $$\label{ref:finite-moment-tests}
 \bigl(\mathbb E\left\lVert PF\right\rVert_\infty^q\bigr)^{1/q}
 \le N^{1/q}\max_k\bigl(\mathbb E|PF(\tau_k)|^q\bigr)^{1/q}
       +\eta\bigl(\mathbb E\left\lVert F\right\rVert_\infty^q\bigr)^{1/q}.$$

For sufficiently small $\delta$ at fixed $L$, let a finite-volume real background have finitely many possible anchors, satisfy <a href="#block:tail-hypothesis" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="block:tail-hypothesis">[block:tail-hypothesis]</a>, and have finite principal norms. Every admissible source jet with finitely many labels then has a transformed centered jet $H'(\zeta)$ and a saved scalar jet $s'(\zeta)$ such that $$\int e^{H(\zeta)}\,\,\mathrm d\mu_0
       =e^{s'(\zeta)}\int e^{H'(\zeta)}\,\,\mathrm d\mu_0.
 \label{block:partition-identity}$$ The equality is coefficientwise in the square-free variables and holds for every disorder realization and every choice of decision seeds. Consequently the normalized source partition function is $$\frac{\int e^{H(\zeta)}\,\,\mathrm d\mu_0}{\int e^{H(0)}\,\,\mathrm d\mu_0}
 =e^{s'(\zeta)-s'(0)}
   \frac{\int e^{H'(\zeta)}\,\,\mathrm d\mu_0}{\int e^{H'(0)}\,\,\mathrm d\mu_0}.
 \label{block:normalized-identity}$$ All pure bad output is principal, and the linearization at zero is <a href="#block:linear" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="block:linear">[block:linear]</a>.

Choose $L$ sufficiently large and then $\delta$ sufficiently small. Under <a href="#norm:input" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="norm:input">[norm:input]</a>, $$\begin{aligned}
 \left\lVert h'\right\rVert_{v,p}&\le Bs/8+C_L^*B_2s+o(s),\label{norm:high-reproduction}\\
 \left\lVert h'\right\rVert_{v,2}&\le C_{\eta,L}F_*s+\varepsilon_* Bs+o(s).
                                                    \label{norm:L2-reproduction}
\end{aligned}$$ Here $C_L^*$ grows at most polynomially in $L$. For the second inequality, assume that each output principal slot has diagonal second-moment bound $$\label{norm:diagonal}
 \sup_\sigma\mathbb E|h'_a(\sigma)|^2\le e^{-2v}F_*^2s^2,
 \qquad F_*\ge1.$$ A bound on its raw second tensor implies this hypothesis. One can choose $\varepsilon_*<[100(1+C_L^*)]^{-1}$: first use the long-slot suppression, then choose the dictionary accuracy $\eta$ and the mesh threshold $r_0$. These choices do not depend on $B,B_2,F_*$.

Under the preceding assumptions, moments with $q$ source occurrences and at most two background arguments obey the ordinary Taylor rule through total background degree two, with error $O(U^qs^3)$ in the weighted projective norms. Background arguments already present in a tensor count toward that degree. With fewer source arguments the corresponding bound follows by Hölder. Moreover, $$\label{norm:source-difference}
               \left\lVert u'-L^dTu\right\rVert_{v,q}=O(Us),$$ and the output satisfies $$\label{norm:mixed-output}
 \sup_{a,b}e^{vq n_a/p+vn_b}
       (\mathbb E\left\lVert u'_a\right\rVert_\infty^q\left\lVert h'_b\right\rVert_\infty^p)^{1/p}
 \le U^{q/p}\{B_s s/8+C_{L,B}s+o(s)\}.$$ The choices of $p,L,\delta$ and the weights can be made before $B_s$ and $U$. All these estimates retain any saved scalar’s carrier until after its weighted estimate is taken.

The coefficients in <a href="#coef:b" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="coef:b">[coef:b]</a>–<a href="#coef:c" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="coef:c">[coef:c]</a> satisfy $$\begin{aligned}
 \sum_{j<J}b_j&=-4\pi a^2\log r_J+o(\log r_J),\label{coef:bsum}\\
 \sum_{j<J}c_{j,q}(y)&=\binom q2\frac{\pi a^2}{2}\log r_J
                         +o(\log r_J),\label{coef:csum}
\end{aligned}$$ where the second error is uniform in $y$ for each fixed $q$. Equivalently, their Cesàro means are $-4\pi a^2\log L$ and $\binom q2(\pi a^2/2)\log L$.

There are fixed constants $X,C$ and $\rho<1$ such that, for sufficiently small $D>0$, every trajectory kept through scale $N$ satisfies the background tail and moment bounds of Section <a href="#sec:norms" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:norms">4</a> with $s=s_j$, and $$\label{flow:rough}
 \left\lVert m_j\right\rVert+\left\lVert K_j\right\rVert\le CG_j,\qquad
 \left\lVert W_j\right\rVert+\left\lVert Y_j\right\rVert\le CG_j^2,\qquad
 \tfrac12G_j<g_j<2G_j .$$ The tensor and moment bounds also hold for the tested next step, with a larger fixed constant for its mean. Moreover, for $1\le j\le N$, $$\begin{aligned}
 K_j-g_jV_j&=O\bigl(G_j(G_j+\rho^j)\bigr),\label{flow:cov-profile}\\
 m_j-g_jm_j^*&=O\bigl(G_j(G_j+\rho^j+\rho^{N-j})\bigr),
 \label{flow:mean-profile}\\
 W_j-g_j^2W_j^*,\quad Y_j-g_j^2Y_j^*
 &=O\bigl(G_j^2(s_j+\rho^j+\rho^{N-j})\bigr).
 \label{flow:higher-profile}
\end{aligned}$$ Here $V_j,m_j^*,W_j^*,Y_j^*$ are the bounded formal profiles of Section <a href="#sec:coefficients" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:coefficients">6</a>. For $0\le j\le N$, including the tested next covariance, $$\label{flow:variance-recursion}
 g_{j+1}=g_j+b_jg_j^2+
 O\bigl(G_j^2(s_j+\rho^j+\rho^{N-j})\bigr).$$ The constants are independent of $N$ and of the kept trajectory.

Along every tuned trajectory obtained above, $$\label{flow:variance-asymptotic}
 g_j\sim\frac1{B_0j}\quad(j\to\infty),\qquad
 \sum_{j\ge0}|g_{j+1}-g_j|\le C\bar g.$$ The uniform comparison <a href="#flow:uniform-ratio" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="flow:uniform-ratio">[flow:uniform-ratio]</a> also holds over all $j\ge0$ as $D\downarrow0$.

For each $q\in\mathcal Q$ and sufficiently small $D>0$, $A_{j,q}>0$ and there are fixed constants $C_*,B_s,C$ such that, at every scale, $$\begin{aligned}
 \left\lVert u_j\right\rVert_{v,q}&\le C_*A_{j,q}^{1/q},\label{mark:lq}\\
 \bigl(\mathbb E\left\lVert u_{j,i}\right\rVert^q\left\lVert h_{j,m}\right\rVert^p\bigr)^{1/p}
 &\le (C_*A_{j,q}^{1/q})^{q/p}B_s s_j
       e^{-vq n_i/p-vn_m}.\label{mark:mixed}
\end{aligned}$$ For $j\ge1$ one has the stronger estimate $$\label{mark:kernel-small}
 \left\lVert u_j-\alpha_jw_{j,y}\right\rVert_{v,q}
       \le C A_{j,q}^{1/q}s_j.$$ The notation $\alpha_jw_{j,y}$ places the representative in its marked principal slot. The allowed disorder can be chosen uniformly for the fixed finite collection $\mathcal Q$.

For $j\ge1$, with the bounded formal profile $Z_{j,q,y}^*$ from Section <a href="#sec:coefficients" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:coefficients">6</a>, $$\begin{aligned}
 C_{2,j}&=O(A_js_j^3),\label{mark:c2}\\
 C_{1,j}&=A_jg_j Z_{j,q,y}^*
       +O\bigl(A_jG_j(s_j+\rho^j)\bigr),\label{mark:c1}\\
 S_j&=A_jw_{j,y}^{\otimes q}+O(A_js_j).\label{mark:s-profile}
\end{aligned}$$ Consequently the marked charge moments obey $$\label{mark:scalar-recursion}
 A_{j+1}=A_j\left(1+c_{j,q}(y)g_j+
                    O\bigl(G_j(s_j+\rho^j)\bigr)\right).$$ The recurrence also holds at $j=0$, where its error is allowed to be $O(G_0)$.

For each $q\in\mathcal Q$ and fixed sufficiently small $D>0$, $$\label{mark:asymptotic}
 A_{j,q}=j^{\binom q2/8+o(1)}\qquad(j\to\infty),$$ uniformly in the marked site $y$. In addition there is a constant independent of sufficiently small $D$ such that $$\label{mark:second-uniform}
 A_{j,2}\le C(1+B_0j\bar g)^{1/4}\qquad(j\ge0).$$

Fix a sufficiently large integer $Q$. After choosing the smallness constants of the construction sufficiently small, there is $a_1>0$ such that, for $\ell_J=Qr_J$ and every nonzero $\chi$, $$\label{crit:tuned-homology}
 a_1\le \mathbb E\phi_{\mathbb T_{\ell_J},t_*,\omega}(\mathcal H_\chi)
                 \le1-a_1$$ for all sufficiently large $J$.

Fix a compact parameter window containing $0$ and $t_*(D)$, with uniformly positive, bounded couplings. If $\mathcal A$ is any increasing translation-invariant event on $\mathbb T_\ell$ and $f_\ell(t)=\mathbb E\phi_{\mathbb T_\ell,t,\omega}(\mathcal A)$, then $$\label{crit:threshold-inequality}
 f_\ell'(t)\ge c\log\ell\, f_\ell(t)(1-f_\ell(t)),
 \qquad \ell\ge3,$$ with $c>0$ independent of $\ell$ and $\mathcal A$.

For every fixed $t'<0$ in the parameter window and sufficiently small fixed $D>0$, $$\label{crit:subcritical-decay}
 \mathbb E\phi^{\mathrm w}_{t',\omega}
                  (0\longleftrightarrow\partial\Lambda_n)
                      \le C_{t',D}n^{-6}.$$
